@@ -15,6 +15,12 @@ The batch crosses to Dart through FJS's Rust bridge as a single FFI call: the jo
 
 With the experimental `templates` build option, a JSX block of native elements is compiled to a template. The first use sends the template's shape once (a `tpl` op: nested element types, constant props and static text); each instance is then one `inst` op with the template id, the id of the root node and the dynamic values. Dart creates the whole subtree from the stored shape and gives the nodes consecutive ids starting at the root id, so later updates address a node inside an instance with the ordinary `setProp` / `setText` ops. A reloaded bundle sends its templates again.
 
+### How a node becomes Flutter objects (experimental render path)
+
+A node's widget is normally built inside one element that watches the node. For the two elements that make up most of a screen the framework goes further: `view` is a single render object (`RenderFuseBox`, a `RenderFlex` that also applies margin, transform, opacity, size, decoration, clip, alignment and padding, in the order the nested Flutter widgets would), and `text` is a `RenderParagraph` directly. Each has exactly one element. A prop change on such a node is written straight to the render object, which marks only what the change needs: a colour repaints, a size lays out, and a `transform` or a fractional `opacity` only updates a compositing layer, so a JS-driven animation of those two props runs no build, layout or paint. With templates on, the constant props of a template (decoration, padding, text style) are parsed once per template and shared by all its instances.
+
+Behaviour is the same as the widget composition it replaces, with these exceptions: a `view` keeps its only child mounted when a second child is added (the widgets remounted it), and text inside a `SelectionArea` does not switch the mouse cursor to the text cursor. `FuseLean` (Dart) holds one switch per part; `FuseLean.configure('none')` before `FuseRuntime.create()` restores the previous path.
+
 Events flow back the same way. A `GestureDetector` fires, Dart calls into JS with the node ID and event name, the signal updates, new ops flush to Dart. The whole loop runs automatically.
 
