+## Widgets that render the node themselves (experimental)
+
+`registerWidget` puts a `FuseNodeWidget` around what your builder returns; that element watches the node and rebuilds your widget when the node changes. If the builder returns a keyless `StatelessWidget`, its `build` method runs inside that element (no element of its own). A widget that must keep its own element — it overrides `createElement`, or reads `context.widget` — opts out by implementing `FuseNoInline`.
+
+`runtime.registerDirectWidget(type, builder)` skips the wrapper: the builder is called once per node and the widget it returns is the node's widget for the node's whole life. Its element has to watch the node itself (`node.attachSink(this)` in `mount`, `node.detachSink(this)` in `unmount`, `markNeedsBuild()` in `nodeChanged`). The built-in `view` (`FuseBox`) and `text` (`FuseTextLeaf`) work this way. Use it only for a `RenderObjectWidget` you want to update without a widget build; everything else should stay on `registerWidget`.
+
